DEVICE INSPECTION AND CONTROL (CHECK OWNER FIRST)

PicoNIX exposes device nodes through /dev. Paths and ownership
are platform-specific; 'pins' provides a live read-only snapshot.
Hardware control can harm connected devices: obtain approval,
check wiring and currently assigned pins before modifying output.

DISCOVER:
  pins                 snapshot of pin ownership/status
  ls /dev
  ls /dev/gpio
  cat /proc/net/wifi   network/connection status on Pico W

GPIO:
  /dev/gpio/N endpoints exist for supported pins.
  GP0..GP15 are attached to the 8x8 LED matrix on the teaching
  board; GP16/GP17 are switches; GP18 has a buzzer, and GP19 has a
  servo motor on this teaching setup. GP25 drives the onboard LED
  on standard Pico only (not Pico W).
  UART1 uses GP20/GP21; I2C1 uses GP26/GP27.
  Avoid driving pins reserved for active peripherals.
  Bare 'gpio' is a full-screen editor: DO NOT run via MCP.
  Prefer 'pins', documented gpioctl/pinsctl commands or bounded
  device access after inspecting 'help COMMAND'.

PWM (actual pwmctl CLI):
  pwmctl status         inspect first
  pwmctl freq HZ       sets shared frequency 8..100000 Hz
  pwmctl a DUTY        GP18 buzzer duty (0..100 percent)
  pwmctl b DUTY        GP19 servo duty (0..100 percent)
  pwmctl off           sets BOTH duties to zero
  GP18/GP19 share PWM slice 1 and the SAME frequency. An audible
  buzzer frequency (e.g. 523 Hz) is not suitable for a servo motor.
  Do not change frequency or duty while the servo is attached
  without confirming its rated pulse period, pulse width and travel
  limits. Fractional duty supports 0.1 percent steps.

I2C1 on GP26 SDA / GP27 SCL:
  i2cctl scan
  i2cctl read 0x23 2
  i2cctl write 0x23 0x10
  i2cctl xfer 0x23 2 0x10
  Scan/read/write/xfer must match actual device protocol.
  Use 7-bit addresses 0x08..0x77, max transfer 32 bytes.
  Do not scan continuously or write random registers.

ADC / framebuffer:
  /dev/adc/temp for onboard temperature sensor (read-only).
  Other /dev/adc and /dev/fb endpoints are platform dependent;
  check help adc/fbctl and device documentation before access.
  Framebuffer user tools may require a terminal or own hardware.

After every state-changing command, verify with pwmctl status,
pins, or a bounded read. Never run 'cat /dev/gpio/25' unbounded:
repeated reads can produce an endless stream of '1'/'0'.

Pico W GPIO ownership: see gpio-board. RP2040 GP25 is not the
Pico W board LED and must not be controlled as a generic GPIO.


FBCHAR fixed-display device (/dev/fbchar)
----------------------------------------
Write exactly one printable ASCII character (32..126), or one of the
case-sensitive pattern names: heart, star, smile, sad, up, down, left,
right, check, cross, clear. The command `echo A > /dev/fbchar` displays
A; `echo heart > /dev/fbchar` draws a heart; `echo clear > /dev/fbchar`
clears the 8x8 matrix. The display persists after close(). The device rejects unknown names and does not scroll text.
Read with `cat /dev/fbchar` to list available patterns and usage;
the listing is finite and does not change the display.
Unlike /dev/gpio/fb (8 binary bytes), /dev/fbchar accepts ASCII.
Do not run scroll or other framebuffer writers concurrently. Python
may open the device for writing and call write(fd, "heart").
